// Gris-Échec (Ch2, MAP_GRIS_ECHEC), Secteur Bêta: contenu-jeu/chapitres/02-gris-echec.md. Raw
// concrete blocks, right angles, no trees; giant screens on every facade showing only grey snow;
// streets paved with recycled electronics (circuit boards set in resin); the Place des Pions, a
// black and white checkerboard; the Tour d'Observation (antenna on top) to the north; the arena, a
// windowless black cube whose screens are the only ones working; the closed factory. Industrial
// espionage mood: very calm, oppressive.
// Ground (transparent pixels): General 94, the pale concrete sidewalk; the walls and the cells by
// the Route S2 / Route S3 seams are General metatiles (0, 94) so the neighbours draw them right.
// Footprints keep the map's doors: Centre and Boutique 3x4 (door bottom middle), Tour 4x5 (double
// door, two cells), arena 5x5 (door bottom middle), factory 7x4 (door bottom middle). Sizes are
// kept small and the prompts flat: the secondary's 512 tiles are the budget.
// Features without a prompt are derived art (scratch compositing of the generated ones, see the
// as-built notes): the jammer on the annex roof (on/off), the bin on a white square, the Tour's
// crackling shut door.
(
    name: "SilGrisEchec",
    primary: "General",
    ground: Some(94),
    style: "Minimalist 16-bit sprite in the Pokemon Ruby/Emerald overworld style (Game Boy Advance). Simple, iconic, low detail: big flat colour areas, 2-3 shades per material, a 1-pixel dark outline, no texture noise, no gradients, no tiny decorations, no anti-aliasing. Cold palette: concrete greys, black, with only small accents of colour.",
    features: [
        (
            name: "immeuble",
            cells: ["####", "####", "####", "####"],
            prompt: Some("A brutalist apartment block of raw grey concrete, perfectly square right angles, a flat roof with a thin darker rim, small dark square windows in a strict grid, and one giant flat screen mounted on the front facade showing only grey and white TV static snow. No door, no plants."),
        ),
        (
            name: "centre",
